From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sit: pad frame payloads to a multiple of 8

The vendor RIL pads the payload of every frame it sends. In a capture from
this baseband all 1611 AP->CP frames -- 861 requests, 326 indications and
the rest of the channel traffic -- have a payload, meaning everything past
the 12-byte header, whose length is a multiple of 8, with no exceptions,
while the header's `length` still declares the true size. We sent frames
unpadded.

It is not a corner case: 55 SET_DATA_PROFILE frames per boot were shorter
than stock's, along with 0x0943, 0x0944, DIAL, SETUP_DATA_CALL and the four
AIMS stack ids. The parity report counted them as byte-identical only
because it compares the common prefix.

The padding goes in `sit::frame_header`, which is the one place every frame
is assembled: the FFI's `samsung_sit_build_request` is a single entry point
out of sixty, so padding there left every typed builder -- which is most of
what we send -- unpadded. Confirmed against the capture per id, for example
SET_RADIO_POWER declared 16 / sent 20, SET_DATA_PROFILE 246 / 252,
SETUP_DATA_CALL 983 / 988, AIMS DIAL 691 / 692.

The CP reads `length` bytes, so the padding is invisible to it either way.
The point is to stop being the only thing on the bus that frames
differently, since every unexplained difference is one more thing to rule
out when the CP refuses something.

`length` is untouched: it already matches stock, because 8 + body_len is
12 + the true payload once the body's four reserved bytes are counted. The
tests that pinned a frame's total length against a capture now pin the
declared length and the padded length separately, which is what the capture
actually shows; the one exception is sit-lpa's fake card, which reads the
declared body and so had to learn to consume the padding before the next
header.

One id does more than this: 0x0d0a declares 1800 and is sent as 1988, so
its buffer is padded to something larger than 8. That is left alone here.
